            category: "Minnesota white grape varieties for the Northeast",
            harvest_time_unparsed: "mid-season, a bit before Seyval Blanc and about two weeks after St. Pepin",
            description: "Casscles, Plocher, and Trzaskos describe La Crosse as a sibling of St. Pepin with Seyval Blanc and Rosette heritage. Source notes: moderately vigorous to vigorous and productive vines, solid fungus disease resistance but susceptibility to black rot and bunch rot, mid-season bud break with secondary crop potential, limited heat-unit fit in colder New England and Upstate New York, better fit in southern coastal New England, the Mid-Hudson Valley, and south to New Jersey, and balanced white wines with pear, apricot, melon, grapefruit, citrus, floral, Muscat, and spice notes"
        },
        {
            type: "Grape",
            name: "Frontenac gris",
            category: "Minnesota white grape varieties for the Northeast",
            AKA: ["Frontenac Gris"],
            harvest_time_unparsed: "late mid-season to late, about one week after Baco Noir",
            description: "Casscles, Plocher, and Trzaskos describe Frontenac Gris as the amber mutant of Frontenac Noir named by the University of Minnesota in 2003. Source notes: similar viticultural traits to Frontenac Noir, slightly better fungus disease resistance, high sugars and high acidity, and fruity semi-dry white to copper-colored wines with peach, apricot, pineapple, honey, grapefruit, and tropical-fruit aromas"
        },
        {
            type: "Grape",
            name: "Frontenac blanc",
            category: "Minnesota white grape varieties for the Northeast",
            AKA: ["Frontenac Blanc"],
            harvest_time_unparsed: "slightly earlier than Frontenac Gris; the Frontenac family ripens late mid-season to late, about one week after Baco Noir",
            description: "Casscles, Plocher, and Trzaskos describe Frontenac Blanc as a mutant of Frontenac Gris found in Quebec in 2005. Source notes: similar field behavior to Frontenac Noir and Frontenac Gris but slightly earlier ripening, high vigor and productivity, good fungus disease resistance, high acids, and wines with apricot, peach, pome-fruit, stone-fruit, and melon notes that are often made semi-dry or balanced through malolactic fermentation or blending"
